1.1. Situation awareness in highly automated driving
Endsley (1988) defined SA as “the perception of the environment in the current situation, the comprehension of the current situation, and the projection of future status.” SA is crucial during driving because drivers must constantly gather information from the environment to understand the current situation and make decisions in response to rapidly changing events (Gugerty, 2011; Endsley, 2020). In addition to its role in conventional driving, SA is crucial in HAD. In HAD conditions, such as level 3 automation, the vehicle primarily controls the driving task, and drivers intervene only when necessary. These conditions do not demand constant interaction but only partial monitoring of the vehicle, which can negatively impact drivers’ SA (Endsley, 2018). Therefore, a decreased level of SA during automation can negatively affect drivers’ TO performance, where drivers must reestablish SA within a limited time. Previous studies have examined drivers’ conditions during the automated mode to investigate SA (R ̈ockel & Hecht, 2023). Reduced SA is often attributed to drivers performing NDRTs during autonomous mode (Liang et al., 2021; Amre & Sun, 2021). This decline in SA is primarily influenced by the drivers’ disengagement from the driving task owing to reliance on the automated system (Endsley, 2020). Consequently, previous studies have demonstrated that decreased SA affects drivers’ ability to regain vehicle control (Yang et al., 2018). This is critical when a TOR is issued in emergencies with a limited time budget, requiring drivers to regain SA within a limited time to reengage in the driving task safely. Furthermore, research on SA focuses on the driver’s state during automation, exploring approaches for developing drivers’ SA. Ma et al. (2021) suggested that a two-stage warning system assists in developing a higher SA level than a single-stage system, positively affecting TO performance. Marti et al. (2022) indicated that a two-second interval between the TOR for disengagement from NDRTs and preparation for a TO positively influenced SA compared with being fully involved in NDRTs until the TOR. White et al. (2019) investigated human–machine interface (HMI) strategies to help develop and maintain SA in level 3 conditional automation. SA in the driving context can be measured in various ways (Salmon et al., 2012). One prevalent measure is the Situation Awareness Global Assessment Technique (SAGAT; Endsley, 1988). Originally designed for aviation fields to assess the SA levels of pilots, the SAGAT measures three different levels of SA. Level 1 SA involves the perception of elements; in the driving context, it is related to information such as vehicle location and color. Level 2 SA involves the comprehension of meaning, which pertains to driving behaviors such as acceleration, braking, and turning. Level 3 SA involves the projection of future states. The SAGAT utilizes the freeze-probe recall technique in a specific situation; thus, it is considered a method for measuring the objective level of SA. This assessment technique is widely used in the automotive domain to measure the level of SA, especially during the takeover task (Ding et al., 2024; Kim et al, 2021; Yang et al., 2023; Zhu et al., 2024). Another measure of SA is the Situation Awareness Rating Technique (SART; Taylor, 2017). This measure is prevalent in other domains and commonly used in the driving context (Tanshi & S ̈offker, 2022; Van Den Beukel & Van Der Voort, 2013; Tan & Zhang, 2024). The SART is a post-trial subjective rating technique that measures an individual’s SA with three subdimensions: attentional demands, attentional supply, and understanding of the situation. Previous research emphasized the importance of SA for drivers during the automated mode, which affects TO performance. However, knowledge of the required SA levels and their development during TO remains limited. Therefore, this study focuses on the SA level during the transition of control and its impact on readiness and perceived safety.
1.2. Influence of time and environmental factors on situation awareness
Building on previous literature, internal and external factors during TO events influence the reconstruction of drivers’ SA. Internal factors encompass aspects such as the lead time available to the driver before the resumption of manual driving and the nature of tasks performed by the driver before the TO request, including NDRTs. External factors pertain to the characteristics of the road on which the automated vehicle is operating and the traffic density around the automated vehicles. A longer lead time given to drivers to gain SA positively impacts their SA level during the transition of vehicle control (Wu et al., 2023). Tan & Zhang (2021) suggested that drivers’ SA is highly dependent on the TO lead time in a noncritical situation with a longer lead time, helping them better understand the road situation. In their subsequent research, they investigated the effect of TOR lead time on drivers’ SA in a freeway exit scenario by comparing 12 different lead times in the range of 16–30 s (Tan & Zhang, 2022). The results revealed a positive effect of a longer lead time on the SA level, with a strong improvement during the 8–16 s lead time, reaching a stable level after 20 s. The drivers’ decisions to assume control of the vehicle were mostly made at the end of the lead time for all scenarios; that is, they took advantage of the given lead time to develop SA. Zhou et al. (2021) considered the lead time given to participants to gain information to be the most important variable for predicting SA. In addition, the difficulty of the driving scenario significantly affected the SA. The decision time was correlated with SA; thus, a shorter decision time corresponded to an enhanced SA.
