Samuel et al. (2016) demonstrated that when participants were provided with over 8 s of lead time to regain vehicle control, the proportion of hazards detected was comparable to that in the control condition in which they were manually driving the vehicle. Similarly, Lu et al. (2017) found that the longer the video length, the lower the absolute error in the number of placed cars, and the lower the total distance error between the placed and actual cars. Notably, these effects appeared to reach saturation at video lengths of 7–12 s. Several studies have found that a longer lead time enhances SA (Lu et al., 2017; Samuel et al. 2016; Wu et al., 2023; Zhou et al., 2021). However, such a correlation may not be applicable when influenced by TO tasks. Some research findings have discussed the influence of the TO task itself on the time required by drivers to achieve SA. That is, drivers tend to resume vehicle control faster despite their lack of sufficient SA (Tan & Zhang, 2022) because they consider the physical reengagement of vehicle control to be more important. In the environmental context, previous research found that a combination of road conditions, emergencies, and traffic density factors determines the complexity of driving situations. Jia & Du (2023) studied the influences of traffic density, vehicles in the road environment, and lanes on TO performance and SA. Their results revealed a significant main effect for all variables; the more complex the situation, the lower the SA of the participants. The findings of Kim et al. (2022) are consistent with this notion. Li et al. (2023) investigated the correlation between the road environment and SA. The results demonstrated that road curvature affects drivers’ SA, influencing their TO performance. The SA recovery time decreased with the curve radius.
1.3. Gaze behavior during the takeover for redeveloping situation awareness
Visual perception is crucial for developing SA in HAD because visual inputs typically contribute to over 70 % of the information individuals gather about the external environment. The eye-tracking measure allows for the measurement of the L1 SA, which is linked to the perception of the environment and is the start of the process. The literature has shown that drivers’ visual engagement in NDRTs affects their SA and TO performance (Liang et al., 2021; Yoon & Ji., 2019; Zhou et al., 2021). Previous studies revealed that drivers involved in visually demanding NDRTs had a lower SA or took longer to redevelop SA. Liang et al. (2021) investigated the relationship between drivers’ pre-TO visual engagement and their SA level during automated driving. They found that a conscious focus on eye movements, such as fixation on the road, was directly linked to a high SA score. Generally, a longer time spent viewing the driving scene and more dispersed visual attention resulted in better SA. Zhou et al. (2021) developed a predictive model for drivers’ SA during the transition period from automated to manual driving. Their model showed that the model with 14 predictor variables outperformed the model with only nine eye-tracking-related variables, such as fixations on the back mirror and road; however, the latter model still predicted drivers’ SA at an acceptable level, indicating that drivers’ SA could be measured in real-time using only eye-tracking data. Studies have shown that drivers may look at different areas of interest over time from the TOR. Lu et al. (2017) presented gaze distribution results across four areas of interest: the road center and rear, and the left-wing and right-wing mirrors. The first time the crosshair disappeared, which represents the beginning of the TO, approximately 80 % of the drivers initially focused on the road center. Subsequently, visual attention to mirrors increased, particularly the rear-view mirror. Over time, the proportion of gaze on mirrors decreased, and the attention to the road center increased. Gonçalves et al. (2022) demonstrated that without HMI information, nearly half of the participants gazed at the road center upon disengaging from the NDRTs. However, when the HMI provided information about the post-TO task, such as lane change, the proportion of gaze on the road center decreased, and the proportion of gaze on the HMI was higher than that without HMI information. Acknowledging the significance of gaze behavior in the redevelopment of SA in HAD environments, this study used eye-tracking measures to examine variations in drivers’ gaze behavior in response to internal and external situational factors.
1.4. Research objective
Previous research on SA during autonomous driving has primarily focused on the impact of lead time on TO and SA. These studies consistently demonstrate that longer lead times lead to higher levels of SA. Additionally, TO scenarios have been shown to significantly affect SA. However, it remains unclear whether drivers can achieve an SA level comparable to that established with a designated lead time when given sufficient time to assume vehicle control. While some studies have explored driver takeover response times with adequate time buffers (e.g., Tan & Zhang, 2022), there is a lack of research directly comparing SA in predefined takeover situations with that in free-time budget scenarios. Therefore, there is a gap in the research on whether external factors continue to influence SA even in scenarios with sufficient time for TO. To address this gap, this study poses the following research questions:
RQ1: How do environmental factors (traffic density and road type) and time budget factors (3 s, 10 s, and free) influence drivers’ SA (SAGAT and SART) and perceived safety? RQ2: How does the response time to indicate sufficient SA differ based on traffic density and road type? RQ3: How do environmental and time budget factors affect drivers’ gaze behavior when redeveloping SA?
Accordingly, this study aims to determine how different factors of the road environment affect SA and analyze drivers’ gaze behavior to develop SA. Therefore, we first analyzed the effects of road type, traffic density, and time on SA and safety. Highways and urban roads were considered as road types with low and high visual distractions, respectively. Time constraint factors were divided into lead time (short: 3 s and long: 10 s) and free response time. The lead time for 3 s and 10 s were set because drivers’ situation awareness consistently increases and error decreases until around 7–12 s depending on the contexts (Vlakveld et al., 2018, Lu et al., 2017, Samuel et al., 2016). The major range for time budget from existing studies is located between 3 and 10 s (Deng et al., 2024). This
