2. Methods
2.1. Participants
A total of 39 participants (19 women and 20 men) aged 21–56 years (M = 36.2, SD = 11.19) were recruited for this study (Table 1). All participants were required to have a valid driver’s license and at least one year of driving experience. Most of the participants had significant driving experience and were frequent drivers. A glass-type eye tracker was used, and participants with normal or correctedto-normal vision with contact lenses were allowed to participate in the experiment. Each participant was paid approximately US$ 20 as compensation. This study was approved by the Institutional Review Board (IRB) of the Seoul National University of Science and Technology.
2.2. Experiment design and experiment task
The experiment comprised two sessions. The primary objectives of both sessions were to collect data on drivers’ SA (SAGAT and SART), perceived safety, and eye behavior. As the time budget factor required different experimental tasks for participants, the experiment was divided into two session. The first session was designed as a 2 × 2 × 2 within-subject design. Two traffic density levels (high and low), two road types (highway and urban), and two lead-time lengths (3 and 10 s) were considered. Traffic density was controlled by the number of vehicles in the vicinity of the car in which the participants were riding (Fig. 1). The low- and high-traffic-density conditions involved a maximum of two and a minimum of four vehicles near the participant’s vehicle, respectively. Two road types were selected in this study: highways and urban roads. The highway had few visual distractors, whereas urban roads were constructed with several visual distractors such as traffic signs, traffic lights, and advertising signs. The lead time for TO was controlled by the length of the video. All conditions were randomly provided during the experiment. The second session aimed to gather the time taken by participants to gain sufficient SA. The experimental design was a 2 × 2 within-subject framework (road type and traffic density), excluding the lead time. The stimulus control for traffic density and road type was equal to the first experiment. Two repetitions were performed for each condition, yielding eight trials per participant. In this study, to control the SA level during the automated mode, no road condition information was provided, and participants were asked to be fully engaged in an NDRT. HAD conditions require the driver to assume control of the vehicle after engaging in various NDRTs. This engagement in NDRT might indicate drivers’ low SA level or no SA during the automated mode. This implies that the driver has little or no knowledge or information about the driving road situation. This was designed to overcome the limitations of an experimental setting in which the participants’ awareness of being tested on TO might influence the SA level during the automated mode.
2.3. Procedure
Before the experiment, the participants were welcomed and asked to sign a consent form indicating the study objectives, and only those who voluntarily agreed to participate in the experiment were included. First, the participants were instructed to fill out a demographic questionnaire that included questions about their age, gender, driving experience, and driving frequency. The experiment was then explained to the participants: introductory material was provided as a written handout, with a complementary video explaining automated driving and TO situations. Subsequently, a practice session on the experimental task was performed to familiarize the participants with the procedure and guide them in acquiring the necessary information to respond to the questionnaires. The participants underwent two practice sessions before each session. The participants were asked to wear an eye tracker before the
