each. The items were designed as multiple-choice questions and included an “I’m not sure” choice. SAGAT method has its limitation in that as it utilizes freeze probe techniques, the results may rely on the memory capacity rather than situation awareness itself (Salmon et al., 2009). To overcome the limitation, only information for two-seconds before the video ended was considered as answer. The reason for setting the threshold of two-seconds was from the prior knowledge that distraction over two seconds significantly increase the crash rate (Klauer et al., 2006), which consequently generated a threshold of two-seconds rule such as AttenD (Kircher & Ahlstr ̈om, 2009). The SART measures participants’ subjective SA levels, comprising 10 items under three dimensions: attentional demand, attentional supply, and understanding. Responses were recorded on a 7-point Likert scale. The final score was calculated by subtracting the attentional demand score from the sum of the attentional supply and understanding scores (Bolton et al., 2021). SART is a subjective assessment method that is easy to use and applicable in various fields, but it has limitations, including the reliability issues commonly found in subjective evaluation (Gawron, 2019). In addition, the perceived safety scores were obtained using a single-item questionnaire (“When the video ended, how safe did you perceive the situation to be for driving?”). Additionally, to assess participants’ level of immersion in non-driving-related tasks under each condition, we asked a single-item question with 7-points Likert scale (“How immersed were you in the entertainment video before the takeover (TO) alert was issued?”).
For gaze behavior, data on the proportion of glances directed toward four area of interest (AOI) during the TO period were collected. Previous research has divided the driver’s area of interest into four to five sections, including the road (center) section, human–machine interface (bottom) section, and left, right, and rear-view mirror sections (Lu et al., 2017; Goncalves et al., 2022). While some studies utilize a virtual mirror to display the passenger-side mirror in a single monitor condition (e.g., Lu et al., 2017), others exclude it to enhance realism (Li et al., 2022; Chandrasekaran et al., 2019). Therefore, this study included the front window, instrument cluster, left window, and rear-view mirror (Fig. 2), which have been identified as crucial for gathering driving-related information for SA development. The right-wing mirror section was not included in this study. In addition, during the second session, the participants were asked to indicate when they felt they had fully gathered the necessary information for re-engaging manual driving. This study focused on SA rather than TO performance; therefore, TO measures were not collected.
2.5. Apparatus and experimental setting
The experiment was performed using a fixed driving simulator (Fig. 3). The experimental stimuli were provided through a 27-inch display with a 1920 × 1080-pixel resolution, showing the frontal view and left-side window of a left-hand-drive vehicle (Fig. 2). The stimulus videos were designed using the City Car Driving Simulator. All TO scenarios were recorded from the driver’s perspective in a sedan. On the right side, a tablet personal computer (PC) with an 11-inch display was used to present a video to elicit immersion in the NDRT during autonomous driving. A Logitech Momo steering wheel and pedal system were installed to simulate the driving situation. Tobii Pro Glasses 3 were used to collect eye gaze behavior data. The driving video was extracted from the City Car Driving simulation, featuring a 30-minute drive in both urban and motorway settings with traffic densities of 30 % and 100 %, respectively. The extraction conditions were as follows: in the first session, stimuli were required to elicit consistent responses on the SAGAT questionnaire for more than 2 s at the end of each stimulus. To ensure this, three primary stimuli were developed for each condition, and two researchers evaluated each one. The segment that received the highest agreement among the three stimuli was selected as the final stimulus. In the second session, it was stipulated that the vehicle must not stop for 30 s (the maximum response time), and the driving environment (urban or motorway) must remain unchanged throughout the duration.
