2.6. Statistical analysis
To assess the influence of environmental and time budget factors on drivers’ SA, a three-way analysis of variance (ANOVA) was employed, considering traffic density, road type, and time as within-subject factors. To compare situation awareness between the predefined lead time condition and the free lead time condition, we used data from both session one and session two in the ANOVA analysis. A post-hoc analysis using Tukey’s test was conducted to examine specific differences. In addition, a one-way ANOVA was performed to compare drivers’ SA levels based on time budget factors, with post hoc analysis conducted using Tukey’s test. Gaze behavior analysis was performed using two-way ANOVA to explore the impact of the driving environment within each lead-time condition. All statistical analyses were performed using the IBM SPSS 27.
3. Results
3.1. Effect of environment factor and time budget on drivers’ SA and perceived safety
The analysis of immersion levels before the takeover revealed no significant differences across conditions (F(11, 604) = 0.556, p = 0.864), with an overall high level of immersion (M = 5.95, SD = 1.12) in the NDRT. Table 3 shows the results of the descriptive data analysis of the SAGAT, SART, and perceived safety scores. The highest SAGAT score was obtained for low-traffic, highway, and 10second lead time conditions (M = 0.81, SD = 0.134), whereas the lowest SAGAT score was obtained for high-traffic, urban, and 3-second lead time conditions (M = 0.37, SD = 0.159). Similar results were obtained for the SART scores (M = 29.46, SD = 10.369; M = 4.95, SD = 10.141). For perceived safety, the highest score was obtained for low-traffic, highway, and 10-second lead time conditions (M = 5.36, SD = 1.564), whereas the lowest score was obtained for high-traffic, urban, and 3-second lead time conditions (M = 3.18, SD = 1.668) and highway (M = 3.18, SD = 1.620).
