A three-way ANOVA was performed to examine the effects of traffic density, road type, and time budget factors on the SAGAT, SART, and perceived safety scores (Table 4). The results showed a significant interaction between the effects of traffic density, road type, and time on the SAGAT score (F(2,604) = 10.557, p < 0.001). The results showed a significant main effect of traffic density (F (1,604) = 78.674, p < 0.001), road type (F(1,604) = 6.582, p = 0.011), and time budget factor (F(2,604) = 52.289, p < 0.001) on the SAGAT and SART scores (F(1,604) = 76.578, p < 0.001, F(1,604) = 7.621, p = 0.006, F(2,604) = 68.980, p < 0.001). For the perceived safety score, the results showed a significant main effect of traffic density (F(1,604) = 51.250, p < 0.001) and time budget factor (F (2,604) = 24.900, p < 0.001). The results of the interaction effect showed only statistically significant two-way interaction between traffic and road type (F (1,604) = 21.922, p < 0.001) and a three-way interaction effect for SAGAT score (F(2,604) = 10.557, p < 0.001). We conducted a twoway interaction examining the interaction between road type and traffic density at the different levels of time factor. For 10 s condition, the results suggest that there is a significant interaction between road type and traffic (F(1,152) = 52.198, p < 0.001). During the 10-second condition, high traffic decreased SAGAT score significantly for highways compared to urban areas. While for 3 s and free condition, there was no significant difference in SAGAT score between road type across traffic density (F(1,152) = 0.457, p = 0.500 and F(1,300) = 0.868, p = 0.352). The one-way ANOVA results showed a significant effect of time constraints on SAGAT, SART, and perceived safety. Fig. 4 shows the SAGAT, SART, and perceived safety scores according to lead time. The post-hoc analysis showed that SAGAT scores significantly differed between each condition, with the highest score for the 10-second condition (M = 0.644), followed by the free (M = 0.534) and 3-second (M = 0.456) conditions. The SART score for the 3-second condition (M = 12.26) significantly differed from those of the 10second (M = 23.54) and free (M = 23.64) conditions (Fig. 4). Similarly, the perceived safety for the 3-second condition (M = 3.85) significantly differed from those of the 10-second (M = 4.63) and free (M = 4.94) conditions. In addition, significant differences were observed in the subdimension for SAGAT (L1: perception, L2: comprehension, and L3: projection), as well as for SART (Demand, Supply, and Understanding; Table 5). The post-hoc analysis results of the SAGAT scores showed a significant difference between each time condition for SAGAT L1 (perception) and overall SAGAT score. For SAGAT L2 (comprehension), the results for the free and 3-second conditions significantly differed from those of the 10-second condition. By contrast, for SAGAT L3 (projection), the results for the 3-second condition significantly differed from those of the 10-second and free conditions. The SART scores for the 3-second condition significantly differed from those of the free and 10-second conditions for demand, supply, and understanding dimensions. The same was observed for their overall SART scores.
3.2. Response time based on traffic density and road type
Fig. 5 shows the distribution of drivers’ response times across various driving scenarios. In all conditions, the response time distributions exhibited a right-skewed shape, indicating that most participants responded to TO early, with fewer participants responding later. The mean response time for all conditions was over 10 s. However, the distribution graph shows that the participants were predominantly concentrating before the 10-second mark. The median response time was less than 10 s, except for the high traffic in urban area conditions (D in Fig. 5). Low traffic on highways (A in Fig. 5), low traffic in urban areas (B in Fig. 5), and high traffic on highways (C in Fig. 5) exhibited similar distribution shapes, whereas the distribution for high traffic in urban areas was less concentrated. In addition, the response time ranged from 2.4 to 30 s. These results indicate that individual differences significantly affect the response time. Some participants were not ready to drive 20 s after the TOR, particularly in high-traffic and urban areas. The mean response time increased when the traffic density was high under both highway and urban road conditions (Table 6). The average response times for highways were 10.65 s (SD = 6.277) and 11.47 s (SD = 6.868) for low and high traffic densities, respectively. For urban roads, the mean response times were 10.04 s (SD = 5.748) and 13.35 s (SD = 7.805) for low- and high-density conditions. The two-way ANOVA demonstrated no significant interaction between traffic density and road type with respect to response time (F(1,300) = 2.626, p = 0.106). Examining the main effects on response time, a significant impact was observed for traffic density (F(1,300) = 7.142, p = 0.008), whereas no significant main effect was observed for road type (F(1,300) = 0.686, p = 0.408). These results indicate that the participants required more time to prepare for driving, particularly under high-traffic conditions.
