situation. However, a significant main effect was observed in both cases, featuring higher scores in less complex situations with longer times and lower scores in complex situations with shorter times. Particularly, for the SAGAT score, the results suggest a significant two-way interaction effect on traffic density and road type. Especially, the high traffic density significantly decreased the SAGAT score during highway compared to urban areas. Likely, we can argue that for measuring the SAGAT score urban areas might provide more complex visual information. With regard to the 3-way interaction effect, the interaction effect between traffic density and road type differs across time factors. That is, it only showed statistically significant interaction during the 10 s conditions, where the participants had a longer time to increase their SA level. In this extended time condition, the negative impact of traffic density on SAGAT scores was notably reduced in urban areas. This indicates that drivers maintained consistent situation awareness levels regardless of surrounding vehicle density, provided they had adequate time for preparation but no immediate takeover option. This finding can be attributed to the inherent characteristics of urban environments, where lower speeds create less variability in the driving situation. The reduced speed in urban areas naturally provides a more stable and predictable context for takeover preparation, allowing drivers to maintain their situation awareness more effectively, even as traffic density increases. In terms of response time and SA level, the results demonstrated that complex driving scenarios required longer response times to develop SA. However, these results differed from those of previous studies. For instance, Jia & Du (2023) demonstrated a shorter TO response time and lower SA score for high-traffic-density scenarios. This discrepancy can be attributed to the difference in the experimental setting and the task given to the drivers. This study primarily focused on SA and excluded the influence of time pressure on the TO task; we aimed to avoid the influence of time pressure posed by the TO task and the limitation of the experimental setting involving participants expecting a TO task that might influence a faster response, thereby influencing SA. Therefore, although complex driving situations required longer response times, the SA scores were lower for these situations. A noteworthy result of this study was the participants’ self-response regarding the required amount of time for drivers to gain sufficient information for driving. Previous studies revealed the positive impact of lead time on SA (Wu et al., 2023). In this study, a higher level of SA was expected when the participants were given free time to respond and prepare compared with the lead-time trials. However, as shown in Fig. 4, the SAGAT score was significantly higher in the 10-second condition than in the free condition, and no significant difference was observed in the SART score between the two conditions. These results are consistent with those of Tan & Zhang (2021), in which the SA score was not linearly correlated with the lead time owing to the presence of a convergence point. Table 6 indicates that the participants believed that they had gained sufficient SA to assume vehicle control near the 10-second time interval.
4.2. Driver’s response time according to environmental factors
Our second research question consisted of ‘How does the response time to indicate sufficient SA differ based on traffic density and road type?’ An analysis of the response time distribution revealed that drivers demonstrate different levels of readiness to drive, influenced by environmental factors. In particular, the participants exhibited slower responses to the TOR in scenarios requiring
