substantial information gathering, such as in high-traffic urban areas. This observation aligns with the logical expectation that longer lead times, known to enhance SA (Wu et al., 2023), prompt drivers to seek additional information when faced with a higher cognitive load. In situations requiring the processing of abundant information, drivers tended to prioritize the acquisition of relevant data, resulting in slower responses to the TOR. Notably, the response time distribution was right-skewed, indicating a prevailing trend toward faster responses. However, a notable density was observed on the right side, suggesting that some participants were not ready to drive even 20 s after the TOR issuance. Notably, the proportion of participants exhibiting delayed responses was high, particularly in high-traffic urban areas. This finding suggests that although the average drivers can attain an appropriate SA level within the lead time range suggested in previous studies (12–16 s – Lu et al., 2017; Tan & Zhang, 2022), some individuals encounter challenges in successfully assuming vehicle control.
4.3. Effects of environmental and time budget factors on Drivers’ gaze patterns While redeveloping SA
Our last research question was ‘How do environmental and time budget factors affect drivers’ gaze behavior when redeveloping SA?’ The gaze behavior under various conditions was analyzed. Consistent with the findings of Lu et al. (2017), the participants tended to focus on the front window when presented with a driving situation and subsequently redirected their gaze to other AOIs. Moreover, the findings of this study support the notion that the participants were predominantly fixated on the front window compared with other AOIs, such as the rear-view and wing mirrors (Gonçalves et al., 2022; Lu et al., 2017). In addition, participants’ gaze behavior revealed that drivers, to some extent, were fixated on the instrument cluster when the TOR was issued. This indicated that drivers anticipated relevant information regarding the TO situation from the instrument cluster and directed their attention accordingly. Furthermore, variations in the gaze behavior influenced by environmental settings and lead time conditions were observed. Specifically, under short lead time conditions (3 s), traffic density significantly affected gaze behavior, with high traffic leading to increased fixation on the front window. With a longer lead time (10 s), traffic density and road type influenced gaze behavior, highlighting the tendency for individuals to focus on the front window when navigating urban areas with high traffic density. Notably, an increased allocation of visual attention to the front window when driving in urban areas was observed in scenarios where participants had control over the TO (free lead time). This observation suggests a potential vulnerability to events occurring in other AOIs, emphasizing the significance of understanding gaze behavior under such conditions.
4.4. Limitation
The generalizability of the findings of this study should be approached by considering their inherent limitations. One of the limitations of this study is the methodological constraints associated with the use of SAGAT and SART for assessing situation awareness (SA). The SAGAT, by its nature, has the potential to alter participants’ gaze behavior, as it interrupts the task to query the participant, which can influence their subsequent attention patterns. Similarly, the SART may induce learning effects, where repeated exposure to the tool leads to improvements unrelated to the participant’s actual situational awareness, thereby skewing the results. These factors represent significant challenges in the accurate measurement of SA and should be considered when interpreting the findings of studies utilizing these instruments. Using a fixed-based driving simulation environment featuring a single monitor with a limited field of view restricted the participants from observing the right side of the driving scene, including the right-wing mirror and window. Consequently, the collected data only partially represents natural gaze behavior during TO scenarios. Another limitation is that this study examined two distinct road types (highways and urban areas), considering the divergent visual distractions each presents. However, this methodological choice limited control of the vehicle speed, potentially affecting the participants’ response times. Furthermore, this study exclusively focused on the TO phase, neglecting the post-TO driving performance and driver reactions to potential hazards.
5. Conclusions
This study investigated the impacts of time constraints and environmental factors on driver SA development under HAD scenarios. The first research question examined the differences in SA levels based on traffic density, road type, and time constraints. The findings revealed that traffic density and road type significantly influenced the SA, with a lower SA observed when vehicle control was regained in high-traffic and urban areas. Moreover, time constraints proved significant, indicating that longer lead times were associated with higher SA. The second research question explored the variations in SA levels when drivers were given sufficient lead time to resume control. While the SART scores remained comparable across conditions with free and 10-second lead times, the SAGAT scores were lower than those in the 10-second condition. This suggests that, although drivers may have a lower objective SA level when taking over freely, they exhibit confidence in their TO abilities, potentially indicating a reduced focus on information gathering on the driving scene when not constrained by limited TO time. The final research question focused on drivers’ gaze behavior with respect to different environmental and time budget factors. The results indicated that the drivers predominantly concentrated their visual gaze on information from the front window. Especially for low-traffic density scenarios, the proportion of visual gaze in other AOIs increased compared to high-traffic density scenarios. Overall, this study contributes to existing research by highlighting that the SA development process can differ based on environmental factors and time constraints. This underscores the importance of providing TOR strategies based on specific TO scenarios. Moreover, the findings of this study have implications for designing effective interventions and strategies to enhance drivers’ readiness during transitions from automated to manual driving. Nonetheless, future studies should address the 